;;; An hrule whose `:match` is `()` proposes nothing — M1f **W31**, register
;;; row `W31/empty-match-hrule` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1f.5.0 (2026-11-08) from S1f.5.9's case (P1f.5's F12), filed `open` and
;;; owned by S1f.5.9, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 on F12.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, an hrule with no premise proposing `(q b)`, and a solve
;;; form naming it as the hypothesis rung.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An hrule's empty `:match` fires as a rule's does (the user, 2026-11-07:
;;; *"2 same"*), so it proposes `(q b)`, as `:match (p ?x)` would: H is one
;;; candidate, nothing refutes it, and R is ∅ and `{(q b)}`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The hrule proposes nothing: *no candidate: R is {∅} or empty, decided by
;;; root alone*, and R is {∅}, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`
;;; at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── Since M1f S1f.5.9 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An hrule's `:match ()` matches once since T1f.5.9.2 (2026-11-09), so `h`
;;; proposes `(q b)`: H is one candidate and R is ∅ and `{(q b)}`, the answer
;;; above by hand. The register's `now` is that answer; the section above is
;;; the record of the one it replaced.

(relation q Node) (relation p Node)
(p a)
(hrule h () :match () :assert (q b))
(solve :hrules (h))
